media: h713-hdmirx: withhold a timing the firmware's window contradicts

A 1024x768 source on 21.09.2026 gave a picture that was sheared, not a
console: INCAP active read 1024x768, total 1344x806 and rowbyte 0x40, which
is a clean lock on the firmware's own table row 49, while the DE picture
scaler's input window (0x05180034) still read 1364x768 from the 1366x768 mode
before it. The driver reported the timing, h713-tv put the ring on the plane,
and what the panel was fed from was still the old window.

T1 found why: the window manager commits its five nodes one at a time
(sub_8B1A8220), each gated on its own bypass byte at node+4, so the capture
node can be written while the picture node is skipped. It is not a table miss
and not a lock failure - the firmware locked - it is a window node that was
not re-committed, and nothing on the ARM side can make it commit.

What the ARM side can do is not report a timing while the two disagree. Where
the firmware does not scale in its capture path - pitch >= ALIGN(active
width, 16), which is every mode on the HY310 - the ring is INCAP active, and
the scaler's input window has to name the same picture: the width to within
one rowbyte unit, the height to within two lines. Otherwise QUERY_DV_TIMINGS
answers -ENOLCK and one ratelimited line says what was seen, "firmware window
stale: scaler 1364x768, ring 1024x768". The console then stays instead of a
sheared picture, and h713-tv's 500 ms re-ask (0136f) catches the moment the
firmware does write the node.

Three limits, all deliberate. Without allwinner,scaler (0136g) in the tree
there is no veto and the behaviour is exactly today's. Where the firmware
does scale - a panel smaller than the source, the HY300 Pro - the same
register is already the ring's own cross-check in
h713_hdmirx_ring_geometry(), and that is left as K3v2 wrote it. And an empty
window is not a stale window: it means the firmware has committed no picture
node in this boot at all, and vetoing on that would hold the console up
through the first lock of every boot.

Follow-up to 0136i; 0136f and 0136g stay byte-identical. Host model (the veto
as a table of ring geometries against window words, the 1024x768 case among
them) in umbau/work/T2/model.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

diff -ruN a/drivers/media/platform/sunxi/sun50i-h713-hdmirx/sun50i-h713-hdmirx.c b/drivers/media/platform/sunxi/sun50i-h713-hdmirx/sun50i-h713-hdmirx.c
--- a/drivers/media/platform/sunxi/sun50i-h713-hdmirx/sun50i-h713-hdmirx.c
+++ b/drivers/media/platform/sunxi/sun50i-h713-hdmirx/sun50i-h713-hdmirx.c
@@ -232,6 +232,14 @@
  */
 #define H713_SCALER_IN_WIN	0x34
 
+/*
+ * How far that window may sit from the ring before the panel is being fed from
+ * something else: one rowbyte unit wide, two lines high -- the same two lines
+ * the ring's own cross-check allows. See h713_hdmirx_window_agrees().
+ */
+#define H713_SCALER_WIN_TOL_W	16
+#define H713_SCALER_WIN_TOL_H	2
+
 /*
  * The display firmware's own timing table -- the list the device locks on, and
  * the only list it locks on: the firmware's match is all-or-nothing and stops
@@ -3332,6 +3340,48 @@
 			     how);
 }
 
+/*
+ * Does the firmware's own display window still describe the ring?
+ *
+ * It commits its five window nodes one at a time and may write the capture
+ * node while skipping the picture node (T1 4b/4c, the per-node bypass byte at
+ * node+4). Measured 21.09.2026: a 1024x768 source gave INCAP active 1024x768
+ * and rowbyte 0x40, a clean lock on the firmware's own row 49 -- and the DE
+ * picture scaler's input window (+0x34) still read 1364x768 from the mode
+ * before it. The wall sheared. A timing reported in that state is a picture
+ * nobody can use, so it is withheld: the console stays until the firmware
+ * writes the node, and h713-tv's 500 ms re-ask catches that moment.
+ *
+ * Only where the firmware does not scale in its capture path (the HY310, every
+ * mode) and only with allwinner,scaler in the tree; where it does scale the
+ * window is the ring's own cross-check in h713_hdmirx_ring_geometry() and is
+ * not second-guessed here. An empty window is not a stale one -- the firmware
+ * has then committed no picture node in this boot at all, and vetoing on that
+ * would hold the console up through the first lock of every boot.
+ */
+static bool h713_hdmirx_window_agrees(struct h713_hdmirx *rx,
+				      const struct h713_hdmirx_ring_geometry *ring)
+{
+	unsigned int w, h;
+	u32 win;
+
+	if (!rx->scaler || regmap_read(rx->scaler, H713_SCALER_IN_WIN, &win))
+		return true;
+	w = win >> 16;
+	h = win & 0xffff;
+	if (!w || !h)
+		return true;
+	if (h713_hdmirx_absdiff(w, ring->width) <= H713_SCALER_WIN_TOL_W &&
+	    h713_hdmirx_absdiff(h, ring->height) <= H713_SCALER_WIN_TOL_H)
+		return true;
+
+	dev_warn_ratelimited(rx->dev,
+			     "firmware window stale: scaler %ux%u, ring %ux%u\n",
+			     w, h, ring->width, ring->height);
+
+	return false;
+}
+
 /*
  * What the source is sending, from the three things the ARM can see:
  *
@@ -3447,6 +3497,15 @@
 
 	h713_hdmirx_ring_geometry(rx, width, height, rowbyte, ring);
 
+	/*
+	 * Where the ring IS the source -- the condition ring_geometry() took
+	 * its "source" branch on -- the firmware's picture window has to agree
+	 * with it before any of this is worth reporting.
+	 */
+	if (ring->pitch >= ALIGN(width, H713_INCAP_ROWBYTE_UNIT) &&
+	    !h713_hdmirx_window_agrees(rx, ring))
+		return -ENOLCK;
+
 	/*
 	 * A lock that survives our own republish. The record at sig_phys is
 	 * the source's only until the display side republishes the VidDec
